      "horizon_year": 2030,
      "context_summary": "{\"city\": \"Mississauga\", \"fsas\": [\"L5B\"], \"scope\": [\"heating\", \"ev\"], \"scenarios\": [\"conservative\", \"moderate\", \"aggressive\"], \"horizon_year\": 2030}\n\nThis study examines the electrical grid readiness of the L5B postal district in Mississauga, Ontario, assessing how the local distribution network can support a growing shift toward electric heating and electric vehicles across three adoption scenarios — from today's early-stage uptake through a 2035 net-zero target. By modeling heat pump conversions ranging from 5% to 50% of homes and EV adoption from 7% to 60% of vehicles, the analysis quantifies how peak demand on neighborhood-level infrastructure is likely to evolve between now and 2030. This matters because distribution transformers and feeder lines serving residential streets are sized for historical loads, and without proactive planning, accelerating electrification can trigger capacity shortfalls that are costly and disruptive to address after the fact. The findings are intended to give grid planners and local policymakers a clear, evidence-based picture of where and when upgrades will be needed to keep pace with Mississauga's decarbonization commitments."
    },
