    "executive_summary": "# Executive Summary: Electrification Readiness Assessment for Mississauga FSA L5B\n\n**Verdict: Constrained**\n\nThe neighborhood electrification readiness study for [[s1|Mississauga's L5B forward sortation area]] returns a headline [[s11|verdict of CONSTRAINED]]. [[s2|All three scenario pairings tested returned a constrained status, with zero pairings rated Ready and zero rated Blocked]], meaning the distribution system can absorb near-term electrification load but will require deliberate intervention before mid-decade targets are achievable. This is not a crisis finding, but it is a clear signal that [[s13|passive grid management is no longer sufficient for the growth trajectory the city has committed to]].\n\n**Binding Constraint: Distribution Infrastructure**\n\n[[s12|The binding constraint across all tested pairings is local distribution capacity]], specifically the transformer network serving L5B. The study identifies [[s3|478 local distribution transformers requiring upgrade]] as the single largest infrastructure gap, and since all three constrained pairings point to the same FSA, L5B is both the geographic focus and the primary driver of the overall constrained verdict. Data on panel-level capacity headroom and affordability indices were not provided in the source dataset, so those dimensions could not be independently scored and may represent additional latent constraints not captured here.\n\n**Scenario Sensitivity**\n\nThe current baseline scenario of [[s4|5 percent heat pump adoption and 7 percent electric vehicle penetration (Today scenario)]] already contributes to constrained conditions, indicating that the grid is not comfortably absorbing even today's electrification load in L5B. [[s5|The 2030 Plans scenario (25 percent heat pumps, 30 percent EVs)]] and [[s6|the 2035 Net-Zero scenario (50 percent heat pumps, 60 percent EVs)]] both amplify this stress, and without transformer upgrades, the 2035 targets in particular carry a meaningful risk of tipping constrained pairings toward blocked status as coincident peak demand grows. Industry rule of thumb in comparable Ontario regions suggests that [[s7|EV and heat pump loads together can increase residential transformer peak demand by 40 to 70 percent above baseline]], reinforcing the urgency of early capital deployment.\n\n**Modeling Caveats**\n\nThis assessment is based on scenario inputs and infrastructure data available at the time of study, and several fields relevant to a complete readiness picture, including specific headroom values in megawatts, customer counts by feeder, and real-time grid latency metrics, were not available in the source dataset and therefore could not be incorporated into the scoring. The constrained verdict should be interpreted as a directional finding that warrants detailed engineering validation by Alectra Utilities before capital commitments are finalized. [[s8|Seasonal load diversity, behind-the-meter battery storage uptake, and future density changes in L5B could each materially shift the results]] in either direction.\n\n**Priority Intervention: Transformer Upgrades**\n\nThe single most leveraged action the council can fund and advance immediately is the program to upgrade all 478 flagged local distribution transformers in L5B, as this intervention directly addresses the binding constraint identified across every tested scenario. Pairing that capital program with a [[s9|default-on managed EV charging tariff]] will reduce coincident peak demand and extend the useful life of upgraded assets, while a [[s10|mandatory heat pump and EV rough-in requirement embedded in the zoning bylaw]] ensures that new construction does not add unmanaged load to a system still in transition. Together, these [[s14|three interventions form a sequenced and mutually reinforcing package]] that the council should treat as a single coordinated initiative rather than independent line items.",
    "methodology_notes": "Per-FSA load impact built from a 24-hour winter peak day temperature curve scaled by ECCC HDD18 normals. Heat pump draws use NRCan ccASHP COP curves with backup engagement below -10 °C. EV charging follows IESO ResLoad evening L2 shape with cold-weather multipliers. Transformer overload heuristic: 75 kVA serving ~40 households, 0.95 PF. Panel upgrade trigger: continuous peak > 18 kW per home.",
    "safety_flags": [],
    "limitations": [
      "FSA topology is approximated from province-average transformer-to-household ratios.",
      "Behind-the-meter generation (rooftop PV) is not modeled.",
      "Panel age and gauge data are not available; the 18 kW trigger is a conservative proxy.",
      "Time-of-use price response (managed charging) is not modeled in the baseline."
    ],
